          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Igor Sikorsky</strong><br>\nIgor Sikorsky was born in Kyiv, which was then part of the Russian Empire, in 1889. From a young age, he had a passion for science and machinery, and this interest was nurtured by his mother, who was a doctor. He studied at the Naval Cadet Corps in Saint Petersburg, but he left to pursue engineering. His early work on helicopters in 1909 did not lead to a practical model, but it set the foundation for his future successes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Move to the United States</strong><br>\nIn 1919, after the Bolshevik Revolution, Sikorsky emigrated to the United States. Here, he started over, since his aviation achievements in Europe were not widely recognized in the US. He taught and lectured on aviation while working on new aircraft designs. Sikorsky's determination paid off when he formed the Sikorsky Aero Engineering Corporation on Long Island in 1923.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Fixed-Wing Aircraft Success</strong><br>\nBefore focusing on helicopters, Sikorsky made significant contributions to fixed-wing aircraft. He designed the first four-engine airplane, the Russky Vityaz, which led to the creation of the first mass-produced four-engine plane, the Ilya Muromets. These planes were used during World War I and were the forerunners to modern-day airliners.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Developing the First Working Helicopter</strong><br>\nSikorsky's dream of vertical flight became a reality in 1939 with the Vought-Sikorsky VS-300, the first viable American helicopter. He personally piloted the VS-300 during its initial flights. This helicopter featured a single three-bladed rotor powered by a 75-hp engine, which was a breakthrough in design simplicity and reliability.</p>\n\n<p><strong>World War II and Helicopter Advancements</strong><br>\nDuring World War II, Sikorsky's helicopters were not yet ready for combat, but he continued to improve their design. By the war's end, Sikorsky had developed the R-4, the world's first mass-produced helicopter. It played a vital role in rescues and reconnaissance missions, proving the helicopter's value beyond doubt.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Post-War Helicopter Development</strong><br>\nAfter the war, Sikorsky turned his attention to commercial and military uses for helicopters. His company developed the first helicopter able to carry more than one person, which expanded the potential for helicopter travel. These advancements led to the S-55, which could carry larger payloads and more passengers.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
